# ── WILDCARD FIRST, then exceptions ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The default line below is load-bearing, and its ABSENCE is what this file used to get wrong.
#
# This started as `*.ts text eol=lf` alone. A narrow first glob leaves every other tracked type to
# `core.autocrlf`, so the file only covered what someone had remembered to list — and a
# hand-maintained list acquires a hole the next time a file type is added. 66 `.wasm` fixtures sat
# unspecified for months, protected only by git's NUL-byte heuristic. Nothing was corrupt (verified
# 2026-08-27, 40 sampled, zero blob-vs-worktree mismatches), which is luck holding, not a design.
#
# The transferable rule, from the binaryang team who hit the same shape: **lead with the wildcard and
# subtract exceptions.** A default that covers everything cannot develop a hole; a list can.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ── Binary: subtracted from the default above (later rules win) ─────────────────────────────────
# Marked EXPLICITLY rather than left to git's guess, which reads only the first 8000 bytes for a NUL.
# A WebAssembly binary's magic ends in one, so the heuristic has always won here — but it is a
# prefix scan, and a mangled `.wasm` surfaces as a malformed module far from its cause.
*.wasm binary

# ── Text kept explicit for readers, though the wildcard now covers them ─────────────────────────
# `deno fmt` emits LF and reports a CRLF file as "Text differed by line endings", while this
# machine's git runs with core.autocrlf=true and hands out CRLF on every checkout. Those two fight
# each other, which is why the "main.ts + src/ is fmt-clean" note kept going stale. Every blob is
# already LF (verified: 30 `.wast`, 20 `.wat`, all `.ts`), so pinning changes NOTHING in history —
# it only stops the checkout filter converting them back.
#
# The `.wast` corpus is vendored upstream bytes that must not be rewritten; pinning eol is precisely
# how to leave them alone, as against reformatting them.
*.ts   text eol=lf
*.wat  text eol=lf
*.wast text eol=lf

# ── Two things that bite when changing this file ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Adding a rule does NOT re-materialise files already in the working tree — existing line endings
#    survive until a file is deleted and checked out again. `git add --renormalize .` fixes the
#    INDEX, not the working tree, and it re-applies conversion to EVERY tracked file: run it here and
#    it also rewrites `LICENSE*` and `.github/FUNDING.yml`, whose blobs still carry CRLF. That is
#    229 lines of unrelated churn and a separate decision. Verify with
#    `git check-attr text eol binary -- <file>`, never by eyeballing a file.
#
# 2. Measuring CR on this platform is booby-trapped in BOTH directions. `grep -c '\r'` treats the
#    backslash as a BRE escape and counts literal `r` characters (reads high and plausible);
#    `grep -c $'\r'` without `-U` lets grep strip CR in text mode and returns 0 (reads low and
#    plausible). Only `grep -U $'\r'` — or counting bytes outright — is correct. Measured on
#    `LICENSE-APACHE`: 140 / 0 / 184, against a true count of 184.
